const tipsDB={
restore:{
dramatic:["Lead with your hook slide — the most destroyed angle you can find. Save all other angles for later.","The 'does it start' slide should be near the end. Build everything to this moment.","Show the damage percentage or price paid on slide 1. Numbers make restoration content feel real.","Your final beauty shot should look professionally photographed. Spend the most time on this one."],
hype:["8 slides max for hype restore. Every extra slide after 8 drops your completion rate by roughly 15%.","Show a damage stat on slide 1 — numbers make the transformation feel more real.","The 'it runs' slide should be as late as possible. Build everything to this one moment.","Side-by-side before/after on the final slide is the most-saved frame type in restore content."],
dark:["Shoot at night or in low light — even with good lighting, a dark grade on post makes it hit differently.","Don't explain the backstory on every slide. Let the car tell the story. One line per slide maximum.","The 'it sits in the same spot' final slide is consistently the most saved frame in dark restore content.","Silence works: slides with no overlay text get more saves than text-heavy ones in dark format."],
funny:["The funnier your disaster, the better the video. Don't hide the failures — they ARE the content.","React visibly on camera for reaction-style slides. Your face sells the joke more than the car damage.","Budget fails get more comments than budget wins. If it went wrong, lead with how wrong it went.","The 'it works somehow' payoff slide needs to look genuinely surprised. Own the chaos."],
},
chase:{
dramatic:["The mirror shot with first police lights is the most-saved frame in all chase content. Get this right.","Don't show the car escaping too easily — tension is the product. Near misses beat clean escapes.","End on silence or an empty road. The chase content that performs best ends quietly, not triumphantly.","Nighttime chase content outperforms daytime by about 3:1. The city at night is the character."],
hype:["Open mid-chase. Never show the chase starting. Viewers drop off during setup.","Every slide should feel faster than the last. Rising tension = rising retention.","The speed number slide gets the most saves in hype chase content.","Units crashing or falling behind are the comment magnets. Show the gap growing as late as possible."],
dark:["Use location shots without the car first — empty roads, city lights, silence. Then the car enters.","The 'almost didn't make it' angle converts better than 'clean escape' in dark format.","Show nothing explicitly. Imply everything. The viewer's imagination does the work.","Night atmosphere: desaturated, high contrast, cool tones. Pre-set your shots to look like 2AM."],
funny:["The funnier the failure, the better. Don't try to make chase content look competent.","React to your own incompetence. The gap between confidence and reality is the joke.","'We both got stuck in traffic' style humor outperforms all other comedy chase formats.","End with a twist. The surprise is the payoff."],
},
race:{
dramatic:["The pre-race handshake or bet reveal is the highest-converting single frame in race content.","Build tension before the launch: show both cars, both drivers, the crowd, the stakes. Then launch.","Slow the pace after the race. The aftermath gets saves.","Pink slip content outperforms all other race formats. If something real is on the line, show it."],
hype:["Start with the stat or the result. Explain after. Never build up to the punchline in hype format.","The gap shot — showing your car ahead — is the single most-saved frame in hype race content.","Show multiple challengers losing in sequence. The '3 challengers, same result' format is proven.","End with a call to action. Comments and duets follow."],
dark:["Race content works best as aftermath. Show what happened after the race, then explain the race.","The 'he won and drove home without celebrating' format consistently outperforms victory celebration.","Less is more: 8 slides with heavy atmosphere beats 15 slides with full race documentation.","Night, empty road, two cars. That image alone does most of the work. Don't over-explain it."],
funny:["The gap between preparation and result is the joke. Build the preparation up, then show the result.","Both cars failing simultaneously is funnier than one car failing. Mutual disaster gets more comments.","'Technical victory' — winning by default because the other car broke — is a proven comedy format.","Show your face when the result isn't what you expected. The reaction is funnier than the race."],
},
cinematic:{
dramatic:["Cinematic is the ONE format where more slides = more saves. 15 slides outperform 8 for saves.","The most-saved cinematic frame is always the car parked in a beautiful location. Spend time on this.","If you have a real story behind the car (dad's old car, first car, childhood dream), use it verbatim.","End with a closing line that feels like the last shot of a film."],
hype:["Reverse chronology: show the finished car first, then explain the build. Front-load the payoff.","The build stat on a single slide gets more saves than a 10-slide build log.","Before and after side-by-side is the single most saved frame in cinematic hype content.","Music sync matters most in this format. Cut slide changes to the beat."],
dark:["Timeline format ('2019... 2021... now') is the highest-converting structure in dark cinematic.","Let the car be the main character. Keep people out of frame or barely visible.","No explanation needed. Let the image do the work. Text overlays should be 5 words maximum.","The quieter the video feels, the higher the save rate. Less is more."],
funny:["Cinematic parody works because the format is so recognisable. Commit 100% to the bit.","Made a deep cinematic about my totally ordinary thing is a proven TikTok template.","The gap between the serious music and the mundane subject is the entire joke. Don't break character.","End with something absurd that completely breaks the mood. That final slide gets the most shares."],
},
crew:{
dramatic:["The fleet lineup shot — all cars from the front — is the single most viral frame in crew content.","Show the crew building before showing the crew winning. Loyalty content saves better than victory content.","A crew with a clear aesthetic performs better than mixed fleets.","The 'new city' arc is the highest-performing crew story format."],
hype:["Roster reveal format: each car gets its own spotlight slide before the fleet shot.","Fleet stat (combined horsepower, total value) on one slide consistently gets the most saves.","Individual car spotlights need to feel like character introductions. Name, spec, role.","The fleet-in-motion shot needs to show speed and formation simultaneously."],
dark:["'Last night together' crew content saves more than any other crew format. Loss beats victory.","Show the fleet shrinking: each departure is a slide.","Don't explain why people left. Just show the absence. The gap where a car used to be.","The final two members driving together consistently outperforms large fleet content in saves."],
funny:["'Every crew has that one member' format is the most comment-generating crew content type.","The disaster member needs to be visibly worse than everyone else. Don't make it subtle.","The crew getting lost is a proven format.","The comeback at the end flips the tone and saves the video."],
},
drift:{
dramatic:["The entry angle — car fully committed before the apex — is the single most viral drift frame.","3 angles of the same drift outperforms 3 different drifts.","The spec card slide gets more saves than any description text.","If you drifted a car nobody expected could drift, open with that claim and prove it immediately."],
hype:["Lead with smoke. If your hook slide doesn't have tyre smoke visible, reshoot it.","Entry speed numbers on screen consistently get the most saves in hype drift content.","Side-by-side with a dedicated drift car (and keeping up) is the highest-performing drift story format.","4 different angles of one run beats 1 angle of 4 runs."],
dark:["Drift content shot at 2-4AM with city lights in the background saves more than any other time.","Nobody watching is part of the aesthetic. Empty roads mean the drift is just for the car.","Tyre marks on an empty road as the final frame is consistently the most-saved dark drift image.","Low, cold, desaturated grade. The car should look lonely and intentional at the same time."],
funny:["Numbered attempts are the highest-performing drift comedy format.","The accidental drift — achieved by mistake — is funnier than any planned drift.","React to failed attempts with full camera exposure. Your face during failure is the content.","'The judge gave me zero' style competition videos get disproportionately high comment engagement."],
},
};